Thanks for the PR. What's your use case? When the tubes are known in advance, the usual way is one transport per tube, plus `TransportNamesStamp` to pick the tube per message:

```php
$bus->dispatch($message, [new TransportNamesStamp(['beanstalkd_foo'])]);
```

The stamp makes sense to me if the tube names are only known at runtime, like one tube per tenant. Is that your case?

If yes, I'd push two changes on top. The stamp should contain a string instead of pheanstalk's `TubeName`, and implement `SenderStampInterface`. Without that, an outbox sends the message to the tube of the transport. And the connection shouldn't switch back to its tube after each put. When the connection drops during the switch back, the message is put twice in the tube.

(fabbot needs the leading `|` on each line of the table in the description)

