by nicolas-grekas at 2026-10-15T07:56:27Z

Thanks for the update. Tubes computed at runtime are a good reason for the stamp. Connections are opened lazily, so a transport per tube costs a connection only for the tubes a process actually uses. The worker side needs a transport per tube anyway.

I squashed a few changes into your commit. The connection now switches tubes only when the next put needs another tube. Switching back after each put cost two more commands per message, and a put could be sent twice when the connection dropped. I also added tests for the connection and the changelog line, and a "0" tube isn't ignored anymore.
